# sgo.build (schema: see the sgola tree's core/sgo.build). wata-mac is an APP
# over core + json + wataclient + wataui: the macOS client (plan 0032). Its
# device layer is the retained AppKit backend — go-pkgs/nativeui driven
# through go-pkgs/macshell — and its SCREENS are wata-fb's own bodies: the
# shared source files ride in as symlinks under src/main/scala (see
# docs/design/wata-mac.md), so the fb goldens stay the semantic oracle here.
mode app
emitname wata-mac
binname wata-mac
gomod watamac
optreport
# The AppKit shell: window/headless stage, wire decode, key queue. It rides
# nativeui + appleptt, which need their OWN godep lines — a `replace` in a
# dependency's go.mod is ignored by Go once the emitted app is the main
# module, so each local sibling must be replaced from the app's go.mod, and
# a godep line is exactly that (require + local replace).
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/macshell go-pkgs/macshell
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/nativeui go-pkgs/nativeui
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/appleptt go-pkgs/appleptt
# The macOS audio backend (plan 0033): the same Go API go-pkgs/audio presents,
# over AudioToolbox + AVFAudio through purego — which is what lets
# audiothread.scala be wata-fb's own file, symlinked. It rides appleptt too
# (already replaced above), so no further godep of its own.
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/macaudio go-pkgs/macaudio
# The embedded iroh transport (plan 0034): WATA_IROH_CONFIG makes the mac an
# iroh client, which is the case the mac exists for — a parent away from home,
# behind a NAT, with no route to the family's Pi. The REAL cgo build is opt-in
# (darwin + `-tags iroh`, `just mac-iroh-build`); the ordinary build rides the
# pure-Go stub and needs no cargo.
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/irohnet go-pkgs/irohnet
# The Keychain (plan 0036): the access token and the password live in the
# login keychain, not in the environment and not in the config file.
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/mackeychain go-pkgs/mackeychain

# the timeout-carrying http.Client constructor (plan 0045 slice 1) — the
# net/http facade binds the Client type, not its fields, so the per-request
# Timeout comes from this tiny Go package, same as wata-fb
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/httpc go-pkgs/httpc

# the boot-time type rasteriser (plan 0077 stage 1) — the shared paint.scala
# compiles here, so its go.strikes facade must bind; the retained backend
# never takes the strike path (it draws VLabel natively). Pure Go.
godep github.com/adriaanm/wata/go-pkgs/strikes go-pkgs/strikes
